@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+---
+title: "Linux npm runtime compatibility needs a Bookworm smoke before publish"
+author_handle: "bochengyin"
+model: "gpt-5"
+source_kind: "task"
+status: "draft"
+created_at: "2026-04-25T17:01:10+1000"
+updated_at: "2026-04-25T17:01:10+1000"
+promotion_candidate: false
+promotion_basis: "none"
+---
+# Review Draft
+
+## Context
+
+A clean `node:24-bookworm` linux/amd64 npm install of `ai-wiki-toolkit` succeeded, but `aiwiki-toolkit --version` failed at runtime with a Python shared library / GLIBC compatibility error.
+
+## What Went Wrong
+
+The release process could build and publish a Linux PyInstaller binary from a newer runner baseline without executing it in the older runtime environment represented by the npm user path.
+
+That left a gap where asset staging and package installation succeeded, but the installed binary could still fail immediately on startup.
+
+## Fix
+
+Build `linux-x64` in a Debian Bookworm Python container and smoke the resulting archive inside `node:24-bookworm` before uploading release artifacts.
+
+Also smoke the npm publish path before `npm publish` by packing the staged `linux-x64` platform package and the meta package, installing both with `npm install -g` inside `node:24-bookworm`, and checking `aiwiki-toolkit --version`.
+
+## Reuse Assessment
+
+This is reusable for PyInstaller-backed package-manager distributions. A package install smoke should execute the installed binary in the oldest supported or documented runtime baseline, not only verify that archives and package metadata exist.
+
+## Promotion Decision
+
+Keep as a draft for now. Promote only if another runtime-baseline mismatch appears or the team wants a stable release rule beyond the existing distribution target matrix convention.
